WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.700
Risky Bash patterns usually share the same shape: the script builds a line of shell text and then asks Bash to interpret it.

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:18.360
That might feel convenient because it resembles what an operator would type by hand, but it mixes data, options, quoting rules, and shell syntax into one hard-to-review object.

3
00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.920
Unnecessary eval is the clearest warning sign.

4
00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:29.860
It causes Bash to reinterpret generated text, which means values that should remain data can participate in another parsing pass.

5
00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:39.720
Even when input is not hostile, this makes scripts fragile because spaces, pattern characters, option-like values, and unexpected empty values can change behavior.

6
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.360
Dynamic command names also deserve scrutiny.

7
00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:53.060
A script that lets runtime input decide which executable or helper runs is harder to review than one that maps a validated mode to known behavior.

8
00:00:53.160 --> 00:01:01.240
The same is true for uncontrolled option construction, where values can influence flags or tool behavior in ways the script author did not intend.

9
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.760
Helper wrappers can hide risk if they obscure shell interpretation.

10
00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:16.580
A wrapper that accepts a free-form command string, forwards it through another shell layer, or silently changes quoting behavior makes it difficult for reviewers to trace what is actually executed.

11
00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:20.680
Convenience abstractions should make execution clearer, not less visible.

12
00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.000
The safer redesign is usually straightforward.

13
00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.180
Use functions for known behaviors.

14
00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.760
Use case statements for supported modes.

15
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.220
Use arrays for option and operand lists.

16
00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.400
Keep executable selection controlled.

17
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.900
Validate values before they are used.

18
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.560
Pass data as data.

19
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.280
The review question is not, can this be made to work.

20
00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:48.500
The question is, can this be reviewed and trusted by someone who has to operate it later.

21
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:55.560
If the answer requires mental reconstruction of generated shell code, the design is probably too dynamic for routine automation.
